<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pope Innocent III</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1161">1161</a>–<a href="/years/1216">1216</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Lotario dei Conti di Segni</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pope Innocent III, born Lotario dei Conti di Segni, led the Church from 1198 to 1216 and was one of the most powerful medieval popes. He reasserted papal rule over central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spoleto">Spoleto</a>, launched the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-crusade">Fourth Crusade</a>, which instead took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 1204, and called the Albigensian Crusade against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catharism">Cathars</a>. He sent a crown to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaloyan-of-bulgaria">Kaloyan of Bulgaria</a>, supported the Livonian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> and the Brothers of the Sword, and held the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-council-of-the-lateran">Fourth Lateran Council</a> in 1215.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pope-innocent-iii.jpg" alt="Pope Innocent III" width="320" height="396" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Carlo Raso · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pope_Innocent_III_(Monastery_of_Subiaco).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the papal legate Peter of Castelnau was murdered in January 1208, <b>Pope Innocent III</b> blamed Raymond VI and proclaimed a crusade against the Cathars and their protectors.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-toulouse">County of Toulouse · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Peter II had himself crowned by <b>Pope Innocent III</b> in Rome in 1204, fought at the Battle of Las Navas de Tolosa in 1212 and was killed at the Battle of Muret in 1213, ending the Crown’s ambitions north of the Pyrenees.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crown-of-aragon">Crown of Aragon · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Innocent III</b> (1198–1216) recovered the Duchy of Spoleto and the March of Ancona and organised the state into provinces under rectors.</blockquote><a href="/empire/papal-states">Papal States · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>He took Varna, negotiated with <b>Pope Innocent III</b>, and in 1204 received a royal crown from a papal legate in return for a nominal union with Rome, winning recognition for the restored monarchy just as the Fourth Crusade broke Byzantine power in Constantinople.</blockquote><a href="/empire/second-bulgarian-empire">Second Bulgarian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The hymn has long been ascribed to the Franciscan friar Jacopone da Todi (c. 1230–1306), and also to <b>Pope Innocent III</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/stabat-mater">Stabat Mater</a></li><li><blockquote>Cardinal <b>Lotario dei Conti di Segni</b> was elected pope after the death of Celestine III and took the name Innocent III.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-8">8 January in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Gavignano, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in Perugia, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.7,13.05&amp;year=1196" aria-label="Gavignano on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4868 678 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Pope Innocent III"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5318 948h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5318 948h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.7,13.05&amp;year=1196">What was here in 1196? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Now just as the moon derives its light from the sun and is indeed lower than it in quantity and quality, in position and in power, so too the royal power derives the splendor of its dignity from the pontifical authority....&#8221;</p><footer>Letter to the prefect Acerbius and the nobles of Tuscany, 1198 &#183; tr. Oliver J. Thatcher and Edgar H. McNeal, 1905 &#183; <a href="https://sourcebooks.fordham.edu/source/innIII-policies.asp" rel="noopener">Internet History Sourcebooks, Fordham University</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;We decree that no Christian shall use violence to compel the Jews to accept baptism.&#8221;</p><footer>Decree on the Jews (Constitutio pro Judeis), 1199 &#183; tr. Oliver J. Thatcher and Edgar H. McNeal, 1905 &#183; <a href="https://sourcebooks.fordham.edu/source/innIII-policies.asp" rel="noopener">Internet History Sourcebooks, Fordham University</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The little boat of St. Peter is beaten by many storms and tossed about upon the sea…&#8221;</p><footer>Letter to the Archbishop of Auch, 1198 &#183; tr. Oliver J. Thatcher and Edgar H. McNeal, 1905 &#183; <a href="https://sourcebooks.fordham.edu/source/innIII-policies.asp" rel="noopener">Internet History Sourcebooks, Fordham University</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
